# Work-based commit messages

Every git commit (including when pushing to live / GitHub) **must** describe the work that was done.

## Required

- Use the project convention: `type(scope): description [CHG-ID]` when a Change ID exists (see `AGENTS.md`).
- The subject must be specific to this change (what improved, fixed, or shipped).
- When the user asks to push / go live / push all changes: stage, **commit with a work-based message**, then push. Do not use a generic message.

## Forbidden

- `updates changelogs DDMMYYYY` as the sole commit message
- Vague messages like `updates`, `changes`, `wip`, `fix stuff`
- Reusing the previous commit message when the work is different

## Push to live

Prefer:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "type(scope): short description of the work [CHG-YYYY-DDMM-NNN]"
git push -u origin main
```

Or: `npm run push:live -- "type(scope): short description of the work [CHG-ID]"`

If `push:live` is run with no message argument, still pass an explicit work-based `-m` via the script’s message argument — never fall back to a generic changelog-date-only subject.
